     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a wide grid of exactly 5 rows and 9 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. Each ROW is one character, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes from column to column. Every character faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION, the same for every row. Columns 1-6 are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF COLUMNS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Columns 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: column 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; column 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; column 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite. Each row is a DIFFERENT species; all 5 live in the Bone Desert (Sand made of every creature that lost), so they share that setting's mood and palette family, but each must have its own clearly different body shape and silhouette. THE CHARACTERS, follow every detail: ROW 1 = \"Dune Skink\" (skittish prey that runs away): A small sand lizard with a long slim body, four splayed legs, a long tail and a pointed snout, two bright eyes. Sandy-orange scales with dark brown stripes, an orange-red tail tip. Quick, darting pose. Exact colours: main amber (#e0b060), secondary amber (#7a5a2a), accent orange (#ff7a3a). ROW 2 = \"Bone Scarab\" (small swarm creature that attacks in packs): A beetle with a shell made of bleached bone, six legs, two small eyes and a little skull-like pattern on the shell. Off-white bone with grey shadows, dark brown legs and joints. Its shell has a rib-like pattern and a small skull motif in the centre; its legs are bony and thin. Exact colours: main light amber (#f0e8d0), secondary grey (#8a8070), accent dark orange (#3a2a1a). ROW 3 = \"Sand Lurker\" (charging brute): An ambush predator rising from the sand: a long body with six legs, two horns, four spikes and four red eyes, a wide jaw full of teeth. Sandy-tan hide with dark brown stripes, glowing red eyes and mouth. Exact colours: main orange (#c09050), secondary dark orange (#5a3a1a), accent red (#ff3a3a). ROW 4 = \"Dust Wraith\" (ranged spitter/caster that keeps its distance): A wraith made of swirling dust: a hooded jelly-like ghost body with three glowing eyes and four ragged dust tentacles trailing behind. Dusty beige body, brown shadows, pale yellow glowing eyes. Its body is shaped like a swirling cloak of sand, small stones orbit it, and its three eyes are in a triangle. Exact colours: main orange (#c0a080), secondary dark orange (#5a4a3a), accent light yellow (#ffffa0). ROW 5 = \"Sting Tail\" (hungry predator that hunts): A desert scorpion with eight legs, two pincers and a curled tail ending in a glowing green stinger, two eyes. Amber-brown chitin with dark brown joints, lime-green venom. Its shell is ridged and sun-bleached, its claws are broad and serrated, and the stinger drips green venom. Tail curled high over its back. Exact colours: main orange (#a06a2a), secondary dark orange (#4a2a10), accent lime (#7cff3a). REMEMBER: in every row the legs visibly move through columns 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and columns 7-9 bite (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
     "followup": "The legs barely move in columns 1-6. Redraw the SAME sprite sheet: the same 5 characters with exactly the same designs, colours, sizes, order and layout (5 rows x 9 columns, magenta background), and keep columns 7-9 as they are. Change ONLY the legs in columns 1-6, seen from the side so every foot is visible: column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Make each leg pose big, obvious and different from the others; a lifted foot must be clearly in the air with background visible under it.",
