     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a wide grid of exactly 4 rows and 9 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. Each ROW is one character, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes from column to column. Every character faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION, the same for every row. Columns 1-6 are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF COLUMNS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Columns 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: column 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; column 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; column 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite. Each row is a DIFFERENT species; all 4 live in the Crystal Caverns (Light bends and sings between the crystals), so they share that setting's mood and palette family, but each must have its own clearly different body shape and silhouette. THE CHARACTERS, follow every detail: ROW 1 = \"Shard Beetle\" (harmless grazing prey): A crystal beetle with a slate-blue shell, six legs, two eyes and three pale cyan crystal shards growing from its back. Steel-blue shell, dark navy legs, glowing cyan crystals. Its shell is polished and faceted like a gemstone, its legs are short and thin, and its eyes are small and black. Exact colours: main blue (#6a8aaa), secondary dark blue (#2a3a4a), accent light cyan (#a0f0ff). ROW 2 = \"Echo Bat\" (small swarm creature that attacks in packs): A round fuzzy bat with two big leathery wings, two eyes, big ears and an open mouth with tiny fangs. Dark purple fur, black wing membranes, pink inner ears and mouth. Its ears are enormous, sound-wave rings are faintly visible around it, and its tiny feet hang below its round body. Exact colours: main dark indigo (#4a3a5a), secondary near-black (#1a1020), accent red (#ff5a8a). ROW 3 = \"Prism Slug\" (ranged spitter/caster that keeps its distance): A crystal slug with three soft segments, two eye stalks and four prism crystals on its back that refract rainbow light. Lilac-purple body, dark purple shadows, glowing cyan crystals. A slimy rainbow trail lies behind it; its crystals are clear with rainbow highlights, and its eyes are on long stalks. Slow, gentle pose. Exact colours: main light indigo (#c07aff), secondary dark indigo (#4a2a6a), accent light teal (#7affff). ROW 4 = \"Geode Golem\" (territorial guardian of its patch): A stocky golem made of a cracked-open geode: two legs, two arms, one glowing eye, five purple crystal clusters growing out of its back and shoulders. Grey stone outside, purple glowing crystals inside the cracks. Exact colours: main grey (#5a5060), secondary grey (#2a2430), accent light purple (#d07aff). REMEMBER: in every row the legs visibly move through columns 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and columns 7-9 bite (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
     "followup": "The legs barely move in columns 1-6. Redraw the SAME sprite sheet: the same 4 characters with exactly the same designs, colours, sizes, order and layout (4 rows x 9 columns, magenta background), and keep columns 7-9 as they are. Change ONLY the legs in columns 1-6, seen from the side so every foot is visible: column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Make each leg pose big, obvious and different from the others; a lifted foot must be clearly in the air with background visible under it.",
