     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a wide grid of exactly 5 rows and 9 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. Each ROW is one character, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes from column to column. Every character faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION, the same for every row. Columns 1-6 are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF COLUMNS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Columns 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: column 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; column 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; column 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite. Each row is a DIFFERENT species; all 5 live in the Frost Spire (So cold that even screams freeze mid-air), so they share that setting's mood and palette family, but each must have its own clearly different body shape and silhouette. THE CHARACTERS, follow every detail: ROW 1 = \"Snow Lynx\" (skittish prey that runs away): A graceful white lynx with a long body, four legs, a fluffy tail and tufted ears, two pale blue eyes. White fur, grey-blue shadows, ice-blue accents on the ear tips and tail. Its ear tufts are long, its fur is fluffy, and its paws are big and soft. A light, quick pose. Exact colours: main white (#e8f0ff), secondary blue (#8a9ab0), accent cyan (#5ac0ff). ROW 2 = \"Ice Mammoth\" (territorial guardian of its patch): A woolly ice mammoth: a heavy body on four thick legs, two long curved tusks, shaggy brown fur, a short tail and small eyes. Brown fur, dark brown shadows, icy blue frost on the tusks and back. Exact colours: main grey (#8a7a6a), secondary dark orange (#3a3028), accent light cyan (#c0f0ff). ROW 3 = \"Frost Wraith\" (ranged spitter/caster that keeps its distance): A hovering frost wraith: a ghostly jelly-like hooded body with two glowing eyes, five icy trailing tentacles and three ice crystals floating around it. Pale blue body, dark blue shadows, white glow. Its body is a tattered cloak of frost, its tentacles are icy and jagged, and its eyes glow cold white. Exact colours: main light blue (#a0d0ff), secondary blue (#3a5a8a), accent white (#ffffff). ROW 4 = \"Rime Wolf\" (small swarm creature that attacks in packs): A frost wolf with a long body, four legs, a bushy tail and a shaggy icy mane, two glowing cyan eyes and a fanged jaw. Silver-blue fur, dark grey-blue shadows, glowing cyan frost. Exact colours: main light blue (#c0d0e0), secondary blue (#4a5a6a), accent teal (#5affff). ROW 5 = \"Glacier Crawler\" (charging brute): A glacier crawler: an armoured eight-legged beetle with four eyes, a mandibled jaw and five ice crystals jutting from its shell. Ice-blue shell, dark navy shadows, white glowing crystals. Its legs are long and spiky like icicles, its four eyes glow white, and frost mist curls around it. The body is low and wide. Exact colours: main cyan (#7ab0d0), secondary dark blue (#2a4a6a), accent white (#ffffff). REMEMBER: in every row the legs visibly move through columns 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and columns 7-9 bite (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
     "followup": "The legs barely move in columns 1-6. Redraw the SAME sprite sheet: the same 5 characters with exactly the same designs, colours, sizes, order and layout (5 rows x 9 columns, magenta background), and keep columns 7-9 as they are. Change ONLY the legs in columns 1-6, seen from the side so every foot is visible: column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Make each leg pose big, obvious and different from the others; a lifted foot must be clearly in the air with background visible under it.",
