     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a wide grid of exactly 4 rows and 9 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. Each ROW is one character, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes from column to column. Every character faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION, the same for every row. Columns 1-6 are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF COLUMNS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Columns 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: column 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; column 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; column 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite. Each row is a DIFFERENT species; all 4 live in the Storm Peaks (Islands of rock floating in endless thunder), so they share that setting's mood and palette family, but each must have its own clearly different body shape and silhouette. THE CHARACTERS, follow every detail: ROW 1 = \"Thunder Roc\" (charging brute): A giant storm bird: an egg-shaped body on two taloned legs, two massive wings, a double feather crest, two eyes and a hooked beak. Deep blue feathers, dark navy shadows, crackling yellow lightning glow. Exact colours: main blue (#3a4a8a), secondary dark blue (#141a3a), accent amber (#fff05a). ROW 2 = \"Cloud Ray\" (harmless grazing prey): A manta ray made of cloud, drifting through the sky: a wide egg-shaped body, two broad wing fins, a long thin tail and two gentle eyes. White-lavender body, grey-blue shadows, sky-blue highlights. Its body is soft and puffy with cloudy edges; its tail is thin and trails wisps of cloud. Exact colours: main white (#e0e8ff), secondary blue (#8a9ac0), accent light blue (#7ac0ff). ROW 3 = \"Spark Swarm\" (small swarm creature that attacks in packs): A crackling spark ball: a small bright yellow sphere with one eye and four zigzag lightning spikes. Bright yellow body, dark gold shadows, white electric glow. Small electric arcs jump between its spikes, its one eye is a bright white dot, and the body crackles with energy. Small and hyperactive. Exact colours: main light amber (#fff07a), secondary amber (#8a7a1a), accent white (#ffffff). ROW 4 = \"Gale Harpy\" (ranged spitter/caster that keeps its distance): A harpy: an upright figure on two taloned legs with two big feathered wings for arms, a feather crest, two sharp eyes and a beaked mouth. Olive-green feathers, dark green shadows, lime highlights. Exact colours: main lime (#6a8a5a), secondary dark lime (#2a3a1a), accent light lime (#d0ff7a). REMEMBER: in every row the legs visibly move through columns 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and columns 7-9 bite (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
     "followup": "The legs barely move in columns 1-6. Redraw the SAME sprite sheet: the same 4 characters with exactly the same designs, colours, sizes, order and layout (4 rows x 9 columns, magenta background), and keep columns 7-9 as they are. Change ONLY the legs in columns 1-6, seen from the side so every foot is visible: column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Make each leg pose big, obvious and different from the others; a lifted foot must be clearly in the air with background visible under it.",
