     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a wide grid of exactly 5 rows and 9 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. Each ROW is one character, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes from column to column. Every character faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION, the same for every row. Columns 1-6 are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF COLUMNS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. column 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); column 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; column 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from column 1); column 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; column 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: columns 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, columns 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, columns 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: column 1 squashed flat and wide, column 2 leaning forward, column 3 stretched tall and thin, column 4 squashed flat again, column 5 leaning forward, column 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Columns 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: column 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; column 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; column 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite. Each row is a DIFFERENT species; all 5 live in the Abyssal Rift (Where the world ends and something else is hungry), so they share that setting's mood and palette family, but each must have its own clearly different body shape and silhouette. THE CHARACTERS, follow every detail: ROW 1 = \"Void Mite\" (small swarm creature that attacks in packs): A small round void mite with six thin legs and one glowing eye, its shell like a hole into space. Dark indigo body, black shadows, glowing lilac rim and eye. Its legs are thin and jagged, its shell is speckled with tiny stars, and its eye glows like a distant star. Exact colours: main dark indigo (#2a1a4a), secondary near-black (#0a0614), accent light indigo (#c07aff). ROW 2 = \"Abyss Stalker\" (hungry predator that hunts): An abyss stalker: a long six-legged predator with six glowing eyes, a fanged jaw, a tail and six spikes. Near-black body, black shadows, glowing pink-red eyes and markings. Its legs are long, thin and spider-like, its eyes are arranged in two rows, its spikes are jagged black shards, and its body is low and slinking. Exact colours: main near-black (#1a1420), secondary near-black (#06040a), accent red (#ff3a6a). ROW 3 = \"Dread Maw\" (territorial guardian of its patch): A dread maw: a blob creature that is mostly an enormous mouth full of fangs, no eyes, four horns and six tentacles. Dark plum-black body, black shadows, glowing green drool. Its mouth is so wide it takes up the whole body, its fangs are uneven and long, and its tentacles end in hooks. Drool drips to the ground. Exact colours: main dark magenta (#3a1a2a), secondary near-black (#140610), accent green (#7aff5a). ROW 4 = \"Null Shell\" (harmless grazing prey): A null shell: a beetle with a smooth grey-black shell, four legs, two eyes and four violet crystal shards. Dark grey shell, black shadows, glowing lavender crystals. Its shell is smooth as polished obsidian and reflects faint stars; the violet crystals stick up like antennae; its legs are short and thick. Exact colours: main grey (#3a3a4a), secondary near-black (#10101a), accent light blue (#a0a0ff). ROW 5 = \"Gazer\" (ranged spitter/caster that keeps its distance): A gazer: a floating round body with five eyes (one large central eye), five tentacles hanging below and a toothy mouth. Dark plum body, black shadows, glowing magenta-pink eyes. Its tentacles end in smaller eyes, its central eye has a slit pupil, and its teeth are thin and needle-like. It hovers with an eerie stare. Exact colours: main dark purple (#5a2a5a), secondary near-black (#1a0a1a), accent light purple (#ff7aff). REMEMBER: in every row the legs visibly move through columns 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and columns 7-9 bite (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
