     "prompt": "Pixel art SPRITE SHEET of ONE BOSS MONSTER's WALK and BITE animations for a 2D game. Layout: a square grid of exactly 3 rows and 3 columns of equal square cells filling the whole image; leave a clear empty gap of background between every cell; do not draw grid lines. All 9 cells show the SAME boss, always the same design, colours and size; only its pose changes. Number the cells left to right, top to bottom: cells 1-3 are the top row, cells 4-6 the middle row, cells 7-9 the bottom row. The boss faces RIGHT, full body, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, filling most of its cell, feet on the bottom of its cell, the same ground line in all 9 cells. ANIMATION. Cells 1-6 (top and middle rows) are a looping WALK CYCLE. The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF CELLS 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. cell 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); cell 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; cell 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; cell 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from cell 1); cell 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; cell 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: cells 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, cells 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, cells 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: cell 1 squashed flat and wide, cell 2 leaning forward, cell 3 stretched tall and thin, cell 4 squashed flat again, cell 5 leaning forward, cell 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Cells 7-9 are a BITE ATTACK: cell 7 = WIND-UP, head and body pulled back and crouched, jaws or mouth opened as wide as possible showing every tooth; cell 8 = LUNGE, body stretched forward to the right, jaws still wide open around the target; cell 9 = SNAP, head fully forward, jaws clamped shut, body leaning into the bite (the bottom row). It rules the Maw of Origins (Every evolution leads here. Only one walks out). A BOSS: imposing, ornate and far more detailed than ordinary creatures, with a powerful, unmistakable silhouette. THE BOSS, follow every detail: \"The Primeval Devourer\" (the FINAL BOSS of the whole game, terrifying and grand): An enormous blob of darkness with three heads, nine eyes, a gaping central maw, twelve tentacles, four horns, sixteen spikes, two pairs of wings and a triple crest. Very dark crimson-black body, black shadows, glowing magenta-red cracks and eyes. Exact colours: main near-black (#1a0610), secondary near-black (#000000), accent red (#ff2a5a). REMEMBER: the legs visibly move through cells 1-6 (stride, push-off, foot up passing, opposite stride, push-off, foot up passing), and the bottom row bites (open wide, lunge, snap shut). Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, labels, numbers, frame borders, motion lines or effects.",
     "followup": "The legs barely move in cells 1-6. Redraw the SAME sprite sheet: the same boss with exactly the same design, colours, size and layout (3 x 3 cells, magenta background), and keep the bottom row (the bite) as it is. Change ONLY the legs in the top and middle rows, seen from the side so every foot is visible: cell 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); cell 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; cell 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; cell 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from cell 1); cell 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; cell 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: cells 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, cells 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, cells 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: cell 1 squashed flat and wide, cell 2 leaning forward, cell 3 stretched tall and thin, cell 4 squashed flat again, cell 5 leaning forward, cell 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step. Make each leg pose big, obvious and different from the others; a lifted foot must be clearly in the air with background visible under it.",
     "negative": "no text, no letters, no watermark, no signature, no frame or border, no photorealism, no 3D render, no blurry or smooth gradients, no anti-aliased edges, no extra characters, no cropped limbs"
    }
