     "sheet": "Horizontal sprite sheet of 6 walk-cycle frames of the same character, each frame 96x96 pixels, side view (profile, seen from the side, at most slightly from above) so every leg and foot is fully visible below the body, full body, walking, facing right, identical design in every frame, centred with a few pixels of padding. Subject: \"Plaguewing\", the player's evolution form at stage 5 of 10 â€” Its wingbeats scatter poisoned dust. Appearance: A plague moth-beetle with a fat armoured body, six legs and four large ragged insect wings shedding purple poison dust. Four eyes, curled proboscis-like jaws, a short stinger tail. Dark green body, olive wing veins, purple glowing spots on the wings. The wings are moth-like with eye-spot patterns, frayed at the edges, and a cloud of purple sparkling dust trails behind. Furry collar around the neck, segmented abdomen with glowing bands. Exact colours: main green (#3e8a26), secondary dark green (#1e3614), accent indigo (#a03aff). Theme: toxic horror: dripping venom, warty slick skin, sickly glowing sacs and stingers. It should look clearly more evolved and medium-sized than earlier forms of its line. Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette, cute-but-dangerous fantasy monster. isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). The LEGS MUST BE IN A CLEARLY DIFFERENT POSITION IN EACH OF FRAMES 1-6: this is the most important rule, look-alike poses are wrong. Exaggerate the leg poses like a classic cartoon walk so the difference reads even at a tiny size. frame 1 = STRIDE A: near front leg reaching far forward and near back leg stretched far back, all feet on the ground, a clear gap of background under the belly (this cell is also the character's main sprite, so make it the cleanest pose); frame 2 = PUSH-OFF A: body dips lowest, the near back foot lifting off the ground behind; frame 3 = PASSING A: the near back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; frame 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from frame 1); frame 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; frame 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: frames 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, frames 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, frames 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: frame 1 squashed flat and wide, frame 2 leaning forward, frame 3 stretched tall and thin, frame 4 squashed flat again, frame 5 leaning forward, frame 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step.",
