ard under the belly with its foot high in the air and knee bent, body at its highest; frame 4 = STRIDE B: the opposite pair now leads, far front leg reaching forward and far back leg stretched back, near legs under the body (clearly different from frame 1); frame 5 = PUSH-OFF B: body dips lowest, the far back foot lifting off the ground behind; frame 6 = PASSING B: the far back leg swings forward under the belly with its foot high in the air, body at its highest. Two-legged characters: frames 1 and 4 a long stride with opposite legs in front, frames 2 and 5 the back foot lifting, frames 3 and 6 one knee raised high as the legs pass. Characters without legs: frame 1 squashed flat and wide, frame 2 leaning forward, frame 3 stretched tall and thin, frame 4 squashed flat again, frame 5 leaning forward, frame 6 stretched tall. Tails, wings and tentacles swing with every step.",
