     "prompt": "Pixel art sheet of boundary WALL TILES for the SQUARE arena of a top-down 2D action game, for TWO different biomes. Layout: a grid of exactly 4 rows and 3 columns of equal SQUARE cells filling the whole image, a clear empty gap of background between cells, no grid lines. Each biome takes two rows: ROWS 1-2 = the biome Primordial Pool (Warm shallows where the first hungers stirred): walls made of mossy dark basalt cliffs with dripping water and teal algae, palette around dark teal (#1d4d4f), dark teal (#236b63), dark teal (#1a5a55), teal (#2f8577), darker than that ground so they read as barriers; ROWS 3-4 = the biome Glowcap Marsh (Spores drift like snow over the sucking mud): walls made of giant tangled mushroom stalks, caps and roots, palette around dark yellow (#2a2a1e), dark yellow (#34331f), dark amber (#3e3a26), dark lime (#2a3320), darker than that ground so they read as barriers. Every biome draws the same 6 tiles in the same cells, all from the same high camera looking down at about 45 degrees, light from the top-left. Column 1 of the first row of a biome = FAR WALL A: a straight stretch of the arena's top boundary wall seen from the front: its vertical rock face turned toward the viewer with its top surface visible above it, filling the FULL WIDTH of the cell from edge to edge and the lower two thirds of its height, its base a flat line along the bottom of the cell; the left and right edges are cut straight down so copies placed side by side join into one continuous wall. Column 2 of the first row of a biome = FAR WALL B: the same wall with the same height, top line and straight cut ends, but different details (a cave mouth, a big crack or the biome's signature feature). Column 3 of the first row of a biome = FAR-LEFT CORNER: where the far wall meets the left wall: the wall comes in from the RIGHT edge of the cell exactly like FAR WALL A, then turns 90 degrees and runs straight DOWN out of the BOTTOM edge of the cell as the left wall (matching LEFT WALL A), filling the cell. Column 1 of the second row of a biome = LEFT WALL A: a stretch of the arena's left boundary wall running straight from the TOP edge of the cell to the BOTTOM edge, seen from the same high camera: its flat top surface fills the left part and its rock face drops toward the right, into the arena, over the right third; the top and bottom edges are cut straight across so copies stacked vertically join into one continuous wall. Column 2 of the second row of a biome = LEFT WALL B: the same left wall with the same width, top surface and straight cut ends, but different details. Column 3 of the second row of a biome = NEAR EDGE: the arena's bottom boundary, the closest to the camera: a LOW rim seen mostly from above (the top of a short ledge with rocks and plants), running across the FULL WIDTH of the cell from edge to edge but only the bottom third of its height; the left and right edges cut straight to tile. Tiles of one biome share the exact same rock style, colours and wall height so they fit together into one closed square frame around the arena (the right side and the right corner are the left ones mirrored). The walls are tall, solid and impassable. Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, no labels."
