LL A), filling the cell. Column 1 of the second row of a biome = LEFT WALL A: a stretch of the arena's left boundary wall running straight from the TOP edge of the cell to the BOTTOM edge, seen from the same high camera: its flat top surface fills the left part and its rock face drops toward the right, into the arena, over the right third; the top and bottom edges are cut straight across so copies stacked vertically join into one continuous wall. Column 2 of the second row of a biome = LEFT WALL B: the same left wall with the same width, top surface and straight cut ends, but different details. Column 3 of the second row of a biome = NEAR EDGE: the arena's bottom boundary, the closest to the camera: a LOW rim seen mostly from above (the top of a short ledge with rocks and plants), running across the FULL WIDTH of the cell from edge to edge but only the bottom third of its height; the left and right edges cut straight to tile. Tiles of one biome share the exact same rock style, colours and wall height so they fit together into one closed square frame around the arena (the right side and the right corner are the left ones mirrored). The walls are tall, solid and impassable. Style: retro 16-bit video game sprite (SNES / Game Boy Advance era), a real game asset rather than an illustration: chunky visible square pixels on a low-resolution grid, no anti-aliasing, no blur, no gradients, no painterly or soft shading, flat colour areas with at most 3 shades per colour, limited palette of about 16-24 colours, clean 1-pixel dark outline, hue-shifted shading (shadows lean violet-blue, highlights lean warm), strong readable silhouette. Background: isolated on a perfectly flat solid magenta #FF00FF background (no gradient, no floor, no cast shadow). No text, no labels."
